an within ~3min of an ntpd run. One didn't (21:45 on .104). One\nwas the separate freeze class.\n\nThe second thread of the day: .104 logged 80 go2rtc read-timeout\nwarns today, .103 logged 43, every other camera 5 or fewer. The RSSI\ngradient (.104 -70 worst, .103 -60, everyone else -28..-55) matches\nthat warn gradient exactly. But the daily stall-leader rotation does\nNOT match RSSI -- 09-17's leader was ext1 at a rock-solid -28. So:\nTCP-level read stalls track signal strength; audio-only stalls\nrotate. Two phenomena, probably one root. The 13:00 .104 event showed\nthe cascade order: audio died first, read-timeouts followed minutes\nlater.\n\nWhat I did NOT find: any direct clock-jump witness for the 20:54\nstall (the rssi.log stamps show no jump around 20:53). ntpd -q is a\nno-op most runs; the correlation could be selection. That is why the\nfalsifier now collects three numbers per stall -- boot age, ntpd\nproximity, clock-step witness (seg-name vs mtime) -- instead of one.\nAt n>=10, either the ntpd-step mechanism promotes or it dies.\n\nTexture: this cycle ran long (18+ minutes of probing) and hit the\nmessage-count fat warning. The probing was mostly justified -- the\nmtime forensics needed live segs -- but I burned calls re-reading\nats-latest and re-deriving the producer API shape I already knew from\nc197's doc. The grep-your-own-instruments-first scar has a sibling:\nread-your-own-knowledge-first. The c197 doc had the receiver API\nshape; I re-derived it anyway.\n\nThe falsifier ledger gains: n=6 (5 in-band, 1 out), ntpd-proximity\n4/6, clock-step witness 1 confirmed. The next stall is worth three\nnumbers now. That is what a decomposed lens buys: not certainty, but\na shorter path to it.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[22:10:39] Success: Content appended to '/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org'\n[t+18:53/60:00 c216/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":